{
  "platform_id": "uI9IVadeONc",
  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:uI9IVadeONc",
    "purpose": "Explains the history and design of the chopper mitten: why classic work mittens are yellow, and how a leather shell over a wool liner became the standard formula for cold-weather mittens.",
    "video_type": "explainer",
    "thesis": "About 300 years ago, two traditions merged into one mitten. Indigenous leather-working gave the durable outer shell, and smoke-tanned buckskin explains the yellow colour. Knit and felted wool gave the warm inner layer. The result, a leather chopper over a removable wool liner, has barely changed since.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The oldest intact mittens found belonged to a child in Iceland around the year 900.",
      "Dachstein boiled-wool mittens are favoured by ice climbers for warmth, wind and water resistance, and flexibility. Michael tests them in near-freezing water.",
      "New England fishermen's mittens were knit about 30% oversized and then felted down to size by soaking and stomping them.",
      "Buff or shag mittens from 19th-century New England are described as the warmest knit wool mittens. Michael bought a pair on eBay to donate to a museum.",
      "Indigenous peoples set the rules for leather mittens. Examples are the Inuit double-layered seal and caribou mitten, and the smoke-tanned deer, moose and elk hides of the Anishinaabe and Great Lakes peoples.",
      "The yellow colour comes from smoke tanning of buckskin. Later manufacturers dyed cowhide yellow to signal the same quality.",
      "Buckskin is probably still the better leather, but cowhide and pigskin are cheaper, more consistent and close in performance.",
      "Wool insulates and stays warm when wet but wears out quickly. Leather is durable and windproof but does not insulate. The chopper combines the two.",
      "The chopper (a leather over-mitten with a wool liner) came out of the 1700s–1800s US logging industry. The liner can be dried by the stove or rotated.",
      "Modern choppers add reinforcements, lobster-claw designs and neoprene cuffs, but still follow the same leather-plus-wool or synthetic formula."
    ],
    "outline": [
      {
        "title": "The history of mittens",
        "summary": "Introduces the mitten as an object humanity hasn't improved, using a 1,100-year-old child's mitten from Iceland, and sets up the yellow mitten and the ice-climbing mitten.",
        "first_segment": 0,
        "last_segment": 0
      },
      {
        "title": "Testing mountain mittens",
        "summary": "Tests Dachstein ice-climbing mittens in near-freezing water and explains the felted fisherman's mitten. Also covers the rare 19th-century buff or shag mittens.",
        "first_segment": 1,
        "last_segment": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "The legendary yellow mitten",
        "summary": "Introduces the famous yellow mitten worn in Minnesota, Canada and other cold places, and argues the perfect mitten formula was settled about 300 years ago.",
        "first_segment": 4,
        "last_segment": 4
      },
      {
        "title": "Indigenous mitten techniques",
        "summary": "Covers how northern Indigenous peoples wrote the rule book on leather mittens: the Inuit double mitten of seal skin and caribou fur, and the Anishinaabe smoke-tanned buckskin, moose and elk leathers. Includes a sponsor read.",
        "first_segment": 5,
        "last_segment": 10
      },
      {
        "title": "The materials behind warmth",
        "summary": "Explains why the leather is yellow and how buckskin compares to cowhide and pigskin. Then covers insulation options and the trade-offs between wool and leather.",
        "first_segment": 11,
        "last_segment": 14
      },
      {
        "title": "Modern choppers and formula",
        "summary": "Traces the chopper mitten to the US logging industry of the 1700s–1800s: a leather over-mitten with a removable wool liner. Shows modern choppers that still follow the same formula.",
        "first_segment": 15,
        "last_segment": 16
      }
    ],
